REM ==========================================================================
REM failover.dgmgrl
REM
REM Purpose:
REM   Template for an UNPLANNED failover: the primary is gone and the
REM   standby takes over as the new primary. This is the last-resort
REM   transition, not the maintenance transition; for a planned
REM   change of roles use switchover.dgmgrl instead.
REM
REM Usage:
REM   dgmgrl / @failover.dgmgrl
REM
REM   Replace <STANDBY_DB_UNIQUE_NAME> with the standby database's
REM   DB_UNIQUE_NAME before running.
REM
REM WARNING - READ BEFORE RUNNING:
REM   Fail over only when the primary is confirmed unavailable, or
REM   its writes are confirmed stopped. Failing over while the old
REM   primary can still write is how two databases both believe they
REM   are primary, and the resulting divergence is not repairable by
REM   re-running a command. Depending on the protection mode and on
REM   how much redo reached the standby, a failover can lose
REM   committed transactions; that is the tradeoff being accepted
REM   when this command runs.
REM   After failover, the old primary must be reinstated before it
REM   rejoins the configuration; it does not rejoin on its own. The
REM   reinstate steps are in docs/switchover-failover-runbook.md.
REM   Do not run this against a database you care about until the
REM   runbook has been walked through on a lab configuration.
REM
REM Notes:
REM   FAILOVER with no qualifier performs a complete failover, which
REM   applies all redo available on the standby before opening it.
REM   FAILOVER IMMEDIATE skips remaining redo and opens sooner, at
REM   the cost of more potential data loss; it is not used here, so
REM   the choice is deliberate rather than accidental.
REM   Reviewed; not executed against a live Oracle instance.
REM ==========================================================================

CONNECT /
FAILOVER TO '<STANDBY_DB_UNIQUE_NAME>';
SHOW CONFIGURATION;
EXIT;
